---
import OrcaDocs from "../../../../components/OrcaDocs.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const description =
  "Orca's web UI: its pages and their JSON, the actions that start, backfill, resume, rerun and cancel runs, logins and sessions, a run's Dista timeline and instances, and links to the code.";

const uiPages = [
  ["/", "pipelines, with their schedules, next runs and last runs; its Timeline tab draws each pipeline's runs over 1 h to 7 d, in dataset order, with what started what (/api/gantt)"],
  ["/pipelines/<name>", "three tabs: Overview (the Progress, Config and Code cards, steps as a graph or a table, next runs), ?tab=runs (runs and versions) and ?tab=operate (the coverage calendar, Start runs and Stop runs); a keyed one's keys, done, live and failed"],
  ["/pipelines/<name>/keys/<key>", "one key of a keyed pipeline: its steps in every run it was in"],
  ["/runs", "runs, newest first; ?pipeline=, ?status=, ?trigger="],
  ["/runs/<id>", "a run's DAG in its steps' colours, its steps and partitions, attempts, errors, reads and commits, its source and code, its timeline, machines and instances, and resume, rerun from a step and cancel"],
  ["/runs/<id>/attempts/<attempt>", "an attempt's log, its last MiB, with its error"],
  ["/runs/<id>/timeline.json", "the run's Dista timeline, for Perfetto"],
  ["/runs/<id>/driver.log", "the run's driver output, as the scheduler kept it"],
  ["/runs/<id>/cluster/<role>-<instance>.log", "a head's or worker's output, from an EC2 run"],
  ["/datasets", "every dataset with its writers and readers, and the graph of pipelines and datasets"],
  ["/datasets/<name>", "a dataset's place in that graph, its snapshots, its rebuilds and the form to start one"],
  ["/datasets/<name>/snapshots/<id>", "a snapshot's lineage, back and forward, as a graph and as tables"],
  ["/rebuilds/<id>", "a rebuild's pipelines by level, with their runs"],
  ["/ops/control", "Ops › Control: the machine running the scheduler, the UI and Postgres: CPU, memory, disk, network, containers, and the alerts sent"],
  ["/ops/fleet", "Ops › Fleet: the EC2 instances launched for runs, their cost an hour, runs and errors per hour; on the local runner, the fixed price"],
  ["/ops/workers", "Ops › Workers: each remote worker's pool, image, last heartbeat and runs"],
  ["/watchdog", "the watchdog's rules with each threshold and value, its trips and what each tripped on, and the form to snooze a rule"],
  ["/login, /logout", "a session for the actions, as a cookie"],
];

const uiActions = [
  ["Start", "a queued run over the partitions entered, trigger ui"],
  ["Backfill", "what orca backfill does: a queued run for each partition without one, and with and what is ticked, the done ones too"],
  ["Resume", "a failed, cancelled or stalled run queued again, to resume at what hasn't succeeded; a run whose code has changed asks for confirmation first"],
  ["Rerun from a step", "a new run, trigger rerun, with the steps before the chosen one copied from the old run, so only it and what follows run"],
  ["Cancel", "a queued or failed run cancelled at once; a running one stops before its next attempt, and the attempt already going finishes"],
  ["Pause, Unpause", "on a pipeline's page: what orca pause and orca unpause do"],
  ["Cancel queued runs", "on a pipeline's page: what orca cancel does, over @all or the partitions entered"],
  ["Rebuild", "on a dataset's page: what orca rebuild does, over the partitions entered"],
  ["Snooze, Keep paused", "on /watchdog and the trip banner: what orca watchdog snooze and orca watchdog ack do"],
];
---

<OrcaDocs href="/s/orca/docs/ui" description={description}>
  <p class="eyebrow">Docs</p>
  <h1 class="section-title">The UI</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    <code>orca ui</code> serves pipelines, runs and datasets as web pages, from the same
    <code>orca</code> binary, on <code>127.0.0.1:8270</code> unless
    <code>ORCA_UI_ADDR</code> says otherwise. It holds no state: it reads the tables the
    scheduler and the runs write, connecting to Postgres per request, so it can run beside
    the scheduler or anywhere else that reaches the database. Nothing is fetched from
    elsewhere, so it works offline.
  </p>

  <nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <a href="#pages">Pages</a>
    <a href="#actions">Actions</a>
    <a href="#login">Login</a>
    <a href="#runs">A run's page</a>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="pages">Pages</h2>
  <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`ORCA_DATABASE_URL=postgresql:///orca ./build/orca ui
curl -H 'Accept: application/json' http://127.0.0.1:8270/runs/7`} />
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>What it shows</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {uiPages.map(([k, v]) => (
          <tr><td><code>{k}</code></td><td>{v}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Every path also answers JSON, to <code>Accept: application/json</code> or
    <code>?format=json</code>, built from the same rows, so a script and a person see the
    same thing. While a run is going on, its page refreshes every 5 seconds, and an
    attempt's log every two; each stops once it has ended.
  </p>

  <h2 id="actions">Actions</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {uiActions.map(([k, v]) => (
          <tr><td>{k}</td><td>{v}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The UI never starts a pipeline binary. An action writes rows in Postgres, and the
    scheduler starts the queued runs on its next tick, within <code>max_runs</code>, so
    the UI does nothing without a scheduler running. It binds to localhost unless told
    otherwise, and refuses a <code>POST</code> whose <code>Origin</code>, or
    <code>Referer</code> without one, isn't its own, so another site's page can't post to
    it through a browser.
  </p>

  <h2 id="login">Login</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone who can reach the UI can read it. Its actions need a login once there's a
    user, or with <code>ORCA_UI_LOGIN=required</code> before there is one:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`./build/orca user add alice          # reads the password from standard input
./build/orca user list
./build/orca user remove alice       # and ends their sessions`} />
  <p>
    Passwords are kept as bcrypt hashes made by Postgres. A login is a session cookie,
    <code>HttpOnly</code> and <code>SameSite=Strict</code>, and <code>Secure</code> with
    <code>ORCA_UI_SECURE_COOKIES=1</code> behind HTTPS. Every action is recorded with
    its user and shown on its run's page, and so is every failed login: after 5 for one
    name, or 20 from one address, in 15 minutes, <code>/login</code> answers 429 without
    checking the password. Behind a proxy, <code>ORCA_UI_TRUST_PROXY=1</code> takes the
    address from <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="runs">A run's page</h2>
  <p>
    A queued or running run's page refreshes itself every 5 seconds. It starts with the
    run's duration, links the pipeline's previous and next run (the arrow keys follow
    them), and shows the settings the run had, secrets by name only. A run with Dista
    tasks draws its timeline in the page: a track per process, a lane per slot, spans
    coloured by function, with a tooltip, and wheel or drag to zoom. With <code>ORCA_UI_URL</code> set to the UI's public
    address, an Open in Perfetto link opens the full trace there. Its
    <a href="/s/orca/docs/run#metrics">machine metrics</a> are drawn under its steps. A
    run on EC2 lists its instances, with the number of workers over the run, why each
    instance ended, and each one's log. The step names, the pipeline and its Dista tasks
    link to their code (<a href="/s/orca/docs/build#versions">versions and code
    links</a>), in a new tab, and the headings link to these docs
    (<code>ORCA_DOCS_URL</code> changes where). The repository's
    <a href="https://github.com/magmalake/orca.mojo/blob/main/docs/ui.md" rel="noopener">ui.md</a> has more on the pipeline page's cards,
    the Ops pages and the anchors that link to one chart.
  </p>
</OrcaDocs>
